The assistant looked at her. “You described losing words before Diane opened it.”

“I described difficulty answering while people watched. Someone put that impression into written words on the page.”

Miriam had made the distinction without prompting.

Evan kept filming. “Do you sense a boy or a girl?”

Miriam faced the composition books again. “The image changes when I try to force it.”

“Younger than us?”

“Yes.”

“Teenager?”

“Probably.”

“Former student?”

“Likely, if my interpretation is correct.”

The assistant lowered her hand. “There was a student at my school who froze every time a teacher called on him. We used to say he lost the channel.”

Miriam turned toward her. “Lost the channel.”

“It was a joke. He would tap his ear and say the signal went out.”

“Was he connected to Bellweather?” Diane asked.

“No. Different school.”

Evan said, “But Miriam used the word reception.”

“After you compared the fragments to interference,” Diane said. “The assistant then supplied the channel story.”

The assistant flushed. “I am not trying to make anything fit.”

Miriam rose. “No one here needs to defend an association. We only need to identify where it entered.”

She faced the open shelves.

“A dog,” she said. “Brown, animated. A van or bus. Laughter used to cover embarrassment.”

The assistant gave a startled laugh. “Scooby-Doo.”

Evan turned the camera toward her. “Why does that matter?”

“The boy I mentioned wore the same Scooby shirt whenever we had presentations. His mother bought three of them because he refused every other shirt on speech days.”

Diane pressed the marker. “New detail from assistant. Separate school. No established connection to this room.”

The assistant’s laugh broke. She looked down at the folded blanket against her chest.

Miriam crossed the space between them and touched the blanket rather than the assistant’s arm. “You remember someone who frightened easily.”

“He hated people watching him.”

“Is he living?”

“Yes. He has children.”

“Then we leave him with his own life.”

The assistant nodded, blinking hard.

Evan shifted his camera back to Miriam. “So the cartoon image came from her?”

“It may have. She stood beside me when the memory surfaced.”

“Or the presence used something she would recognize.”

“That is also possible within my framework. The recording cannot distinguish them.”

Abby’s camera remained steady, but her eyes met Diane’s above it.

The return fan shut off.

The paper on the floor stopped trembling. The high ballast vibration remained. Warmth settled beneath the fluorescent rows. With the fan’s masking rush gone, coat fabric and breath separated from the electrical hum. The stacked chairs narrowed the aisle into a channel between hard plastic edges. Dust and dry paper replaced the corridor’s metallic smell.

Miriam turned toward the rear cabinets.

“There is another impression now,” she said.

Evan asked, “The helper?”

“I see a man above tree line. Smoke below him. Straps across his chest.”

Her assistant’s head lifted.

Miriam continued. “He descends instead of climbing. His gloves are heavy. He has come for the younger person.”

The assistant whispered, “Your uncle.”

Miriam looked back at her. “Which uncle?”

“The one who jumped fires. Your mother showed me the photograph at the cabin.”

Evan moved closer. “He was a smoke jumper?”

The assistant nodded. “Years ago. He died after retirement.”

Diane marked the exchange. “Helper identity supplied by assistant.”

Miriam remained still. “I saw a descending firefighter before she identified him.”

“You also knew your uncle’s work,” Diane said.

“Yes. I have spoken about him publicly and privately. His appearance has personal meaning to me. That gives us an ordinary information path.”

Evan lowered the camera a few inches. “You believe he is here anyway.”

“I believe he sometimes assists when frightened people need help. Belief and proof remain separate.”

The assistant wiped beneath one eye with the heel of her hand.

Miriam looked toward Diane. “May I address the young person without a name?”

Diane looked at the anonymous exercise sheet, the stacked chairs, the assistant’s face, and the camera positions. She pointed to the threshold.

“No one closes the door. No claims about a real student. No cause of death. No invitation to follow anyone. Abby records the full exchange.”

Miriam nodded. “Agreed.”

Evan asked, “Can I move for a better angle?”

“No. Hold your current position.”

Miriam stood between the teacher’s desk and the first row of stacked chairs. Her assistant remained near the shelving. Diane stayed beside the open door with the facilities case at her feet.

Miriam spoke in the same calm voice she had used when accepting Diane’s conditions upstairs.

“If someone is listening and school became a place of shame, you can let the moment end without proving who caused it. You owe this room no answer. You owe us no name.”

The assistant drew a breath that shook at the end.

Miriam continued. “If you recognize the man above the trees, you may go with him. If you recognize only kindness, follow that. No one here owns your story. No one here can force you to perform it.”

Evan’s camera motor adjusted focus with a small internal click.

The return fan remained off.

Miriam raised one hand, palm open toward the rear cabinets. “Leave the desks. Leave the faces that waited. Leave the question you could not answer. Whatever comes next, meet it without this room around you.”

The assistant began to cry without sound. She lowered the blanket from her chest and held it at her side.

Diane watched the paper near the desk. It stayed flat against the tile.

Miriam waited.

The fluorescent strip above the cabinets flickered once. Diane looked toward the fixture. One tube had darkened at the end. The ballast hum changed pitch, then steadied.

Evan whispered, “There.”

Diane raised one finger without looking at him.

Miriam closed her open hand.

The assistant released a long breath and pressed both palms against her eyes. When she lowered them, her shoulders had dropped.

“I feel lighter,” she said.

Miriam stepped toward her. “That may belong to you.”

The assistant nodded.

No one spoke for several seconds.

Diane marked the time. The return fan restarted with a dull mechanical thump. Air crossed the room, lifted one corner of the exercise sheet, and pushed it back against the chair leg.

Evan turned his camera toward the paper. “The room changed during the crossing.”

“The scheduled fan cycle changed,” Diane said. “The light also flickered.”

“At the same moment.”

“Record the times. Compare them later.”

Miriam looked at the page. “The ritual is complete within my practice.”

“Can you still sense the student?” Evan asked.

“I sense less distress.”

“Gone?”

“I would not use that word.”

Diane crouched and slid the exercise sheet into a clear evidence sleeve. She wrote ANONYMOUS CLASSROOM EXERCISE — FOUND AFTER GENERAL SCHOOL-DISTRESS IMPRESSION across the label.

Evan filmed the wording.

“That is longer than it needs to be,” he said.

“It is as long as the sequence.”

They left the room with Abby filming the open door and every person crossing the threshold. Diane locked it after the final camera cleared the frame. She tested the lever twice and showed the key ring before returning it beneath her coat.

Evan and Miriam continued toward the main basement room. The assistant followed more slowly, carrying the blanket folded over one arm rather than against her chest.

Abby waited beside Diane until the others had moved farther down the corridor.

“You know how this cuts,” she said.

Diane looked down the corridor. Evan had already begun asking Miriam whether the helper proved that other presences could be guided out.

“Tell me.”

“Remove the battery test, the assistant’s channel story, your source notes, and Miriam’s qualifications. Keep the broad student impression, the paper, the firefighter, the light flicker, and the fan stopping. It becomes a clean rescue.”

Diane pressed the marker on her recorder.

Abby lowered her voice further. “There is another problem.”

“What?”

“Before the formal start, upstairs. Evan said, ‘Diane set the rules.’ Miriam stood beside him. Less than a minute later he used your name again.”

Miriam continued down the corridor without turning.

“At four-oh-three nineteen and four-oh-four twelve,” Abby said. “Both recordings have it.”

“Preserve everything before the formal start.”

“I already flagged it.”

Farther down the corridor, Evan turned and called for Diane to join them.

Abby kept the camera on him.

Diane checked the running time on her recorder, pressed the marker once more, and walked toward the sound of her own name.

## Scene 5 — The Fire Door

Diane followed the corridor toward the sound of her own name.

Evan waited at the entrance to the main basement room with his camera raised. Miriam stood several feet inside, near the floor drain. Her assistant remained beside the south wall, the folded blanket hanging from one arm. Abby had taken the opposite side of the doorway, where her lens could hold Evan, Miriam, and most of the corridor behind them.

“What happened?” Diane asked.

Evan pointed into the room. “She felt the student again.”

Miriam looked toward him. “I said the distress had returned.”

“After the crossing?”

“Yes.”

Diane stopped outside the threshold. “Current positions first.”

Abby named them and stated the time. The main room connected the service corridor to two smaller mechanical spaces. A steel fire door stood open behind Miriam, held against its wall stop by the closer arm. Another door led west toward the former boiler room. Both doors had narrow wired-glass panes. A bank of electrical panels covered the north wall. Red status lights showed through three plastic windows, steady and dim.

The room smelled different from the classroom-storage room. The dry paper odor had fallen away. Warm dust, floor cleaner, and a faint sour trace came from the drain.

Diane pointed toward the floor. “The trap may be low.”

Evan looked at Miriam. “Is that what you feel?”

Miriam inhaled once. “The odor has a physical source. The impression remains separate.”

Diane pressed the marker on her recorder. She kept both boots outside the blue tape line Abby had placed across the threshold.

Miriam turned toward the west door. “This person carries anger differently from the one we addressed.”

“The same student?” Evan asked.

“I cannot establish that.”

“You said the distress returned.”

“I said distress returned.”

Miriam’s assistant shifted the blanket onto her other arm. “It feels colder here.”

Diane checked the wall thermometer. “Two degrees warmer than the classroom.”

“My face feels cold.”

“Record the sensation and location,” Diane said.

Abby repeated it into her microphone.

Miriam moved toward the floor drain, then stopped. “Someone expected ridicule before anyone spoke. He entered rooms already prepared for it.”

Evan lowered his voice. “A boy.”

Miriam looked at him. “That image is stronger here.”

Diane marked the sequence.

“Teenage?” Evan asked.

“Older child or young adult.”

“Student at Bellweather?”

“School remains part of it.”

The assistant said, “The note could belong to him.”

Diane answered from the corridor. “The note remains anonymous.”

“I know. I only mean the feeling matches.”

Miriam pressed two fingertips against the center of her sternum. “There is impact. Sudden movement. Glass.”

“A crash,” Evan said.

Miriam’s eyes stayed closed. “Possibly.”

“A car crash?”

“Possibly.”

Evan turned the camera toward Diane for a moment, then back to Miriam. “We heard about a student who died in a wreck near the school.”

Diane looked at Abby.

Abby pressed her marker. “Prior-case disclosure at four-forty-seven and six seconds.”

Miriam opened her eyes. “That information changes the reading.”

“But you mentioned impact first,” Evan said.

